\section{A Functional Update Does Not Require a Copy}

In a pure array interface, \texttt{set(a, i, v)} returns a new array value with element \(i\) replaced. The old value \texttt{a} must remain available unchanged if a client retains it. A flat immutable implementation can meet the specification by copying the entire buffer, but a chain of \(m\) updates to an \(n\)-element array then costs \(O(mn)\). A persistent tree or chunked representation shares unchanged portions at the cost of an extra layer of indexing. Where the compiler can establish that the old value is no longer observable, reusing a flat buffer gives constant-time updates and keeps the cache-friendly layout.

Edsko de Vries, Rinus Plasmeijer, and David M. Abrahamson's \emph{Uniqueness Typing Simplified} represents sharing information as a type attribute and gives a soundness result for the core with respect to a call-by-need calculus. In a simplified strict array interface, \texttt{write} consumes a unique array and returns its successor. A \texttt{read} can borrow the array for a scope that ends before the next write, or consume and return the array alongside the scalar read. These are two possible interface designs, not a claim that all uniqueness systems expose borrowing. A checker must account for references in closures and intermediate objects, not just count textual occurrences of the array's name.

\section{Worked Example: Old and New Versions}

\begin{verbatim}
u0 = alloc_unique(3, 0)
u1 = write(u0, 1, 7)
u2 = write(u1, 2, 9)
freeze(u2)                   -- [0, 7, 9]
\end{verbatim}

The lowering may map all three versions to one address: after each write the old capability is dead, and the next one is live. \texttt{freeze} here consumes the final capability and exposes an immutable array without copying. Until then, the compiler keeps the versions distinct in its IR, so a subsequent operation cannot accidentally observe the wrong version merely because the generated machine code uses one address. As a negative case, add \texttt{k = fun () -> index(u1, 2)} between the two writes and invoke \texttt{k} after the second write. Under persistent array semantics the answer would be \(0\), not \(9\). The unique interface must reject keeping that alias, or a compiler implementing an ordinary persistent interface must fall back to copying or another persistent representation.

Views require special attention. A live slice of \texttt{u1} may alias its backing buffer even though it has a different descriptor. A whole-buffer uniqueness checker must treat the slice as an alias for the duration of its borrow; an implementation supporting independently mutable slices needs a stronger disjointness fact. Also perform bounds checks before any destructive update that can fail. If an exception handler can access the old version, the exceptional path must either preserve it or be rejected by the ownership rules.

\section{Regions Answer a Different Question}

A region groups allocations with a common reclamation point. In \emph{A Simple Region Inference Algorithm for a First-Order Functional Language}, Manuel Montenegro, Ricardo Pe\~na, and Clara Segura describe Safe, where each call has a working region released at return and the compiler infers region parameters for data that must live elsewhere. In a pipeline inspired by this design, a temporary work array can live in a callee's local region while the output buffer is allocated in a region supplied by the caller. Returning a frozen view into the callee region is illegal even if that view is unique. Conversely, two arrays safely stored for a long time in the same region are not necessarily aliases, and sharing a region does not establish the uniqueness of either one.

The region invariant is that every surviving root and every object it can reach refers only to live storage. A useful paired test returns an array into a caller region successfully, then changes the program to return a view into the work region and checks that inference promotes or rejects the unsafe allocation. Use separate test cases for writes with live aliases and for reclamation with live references. As in the memory IR proposed in \emph{The Modern Compiler Dossier}, Chapter~17, explicit memory versions help express which state each operation observes. In an array compiler, versions, uniqueness facts, and region lifetimes each justify a different part of the lowering.

